# The NEGATIVE direction, and it is a different fault from a leak: a `consumes`
# with no `acquires` behind it, so a program hands back what the count never saw
# arrive (M-marked-acquisition, panel 148 R2).
#
# **This case exists because it happened.** `examples/curl/main.hero` has
# carried `consumes` since panel 145 landed it on 2026-09-13, and the first
# counter built for this milestone killed that correct program at exit 134 with
# a message saying **`1 C handle(s) never given back`** — the exact opposite of
# what had occurred. One counter, two sentences: positive accuses the PROGRAM,
# negative accuses the BINDING.
#
# **THE PROGRAM BELOW WAS REWRITTEN ON 2026-09-14, one step after it was
# written, and the rewrite is the finding.** It used to declare
# `function slot_open(n: i64) -> Slot` unmarked and call it directly. Then
# `check/acquiring.hero` landed and refuses exactly that at COMPILE time, so
# this case could no longer reach the runtime at all: three suites went red —
# `run`, `emission` and `determinism` — and the full net before the push is what
# read them.
#
# The shape below is the one that still arrives: the handle comes back inside a
# record FIELD. `unmarked_handle_producer` reads results and `@` out-parameters
# and does not look into a returned record, so `pair_make` carries no mark, the
# compiler says nothing, and the count is what catches it. That gap is filed as
# **defect 033** rather than closed here, because widening what a diagnostic
# refuses is a diagnostic CLASS and goes to the panel.
#
# The program below is right and its binding is not, which is the whole point:
# the diagnosis has to name the declaration, not the code.

extern "abort-handle-given-back-unmarked.h"
    record Slot tag Slot

    # The handle rides home in a field, which is where the rule cannot see it.
    record Pair
        s: Slot
        k: i64
    function slot_close(s: Slot consumes)
    function slot_value(s: Slot) -> i64
    function pair_make(n: i64) -> Pair

function main()
    p = pair_make(n: 7)
    print(slot_value(s: p.s))
    slot_close(p.s)
